integrated on-ramp partners rather than controlled directly.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Availability, pricing, and policy decisions of integrated on-ramps", "Primary Function": "Fiat → Crypto Access (Wallet-Embedded)", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "Inherited from integrated providers (MoonPay, Ramp, Transak, Banxa, etc.)", "Primary Use Case": "First-time onboarding into Ethereum self-custody; mobile wallet funding", "Primary User Segment": "Retail users; mobile-first and global users onboarding into self-custody", "Primary Value Flow": "Fiat → (Partner On-Ramp) → ETH / Stablecoin → Ethereum", "Reason for Inclusion": "Trust Wallet qualifies as a Global On-Ramp because its wallet-embedded on-ramp functionality enables fiat → Ethereum access at global scale, even though fiat processing is delegated to third-party providers.", "Regional Restrictions": "Yes (inherits jurisdictional limits from underlying on-ramp providers)", "Regulatory Model": "Partner-Licensed (Trust Wallet does not directly process fiat)", "Role Relative to Ethereum": "Access Orchestration Layer (Wallet-Native)", "Supported Crypto Assets": "ETH, major stablecoins (USDC, USDT), broad ERC-20 and multi-chain assets", "Supported Fiat Currencies": "USD, EUR, GBP, and numerous local currencies (via partners)", "Supported Payment Methods": "Credit/Debit Cards, Bank Transfers, Local Payment Rails (via integrated on-ramps)", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum Access": "High"}, "source_label": "Global On-Ramps"}'::jsonb),
  ('paypal-crypto-on-ramp', 'PayPal (Crypto On-Ramp)', 'monetary-access-rails', 'global-on-ramps', 'https://www.paypal.com/crypto', ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "PayPal’s crypto on-ramp allows users to purchase Ethereum assets directly from their PayPal balance or linked payment methods, leveraging PayPal’s global payments network to provide mainstream access to Ethereum.", "extras": {"Access Bottleneck Type": "Regulation + Platform Policy + Custody Control", "Conversion Control Model": "Centralized", "Custody of Funds": "PayPal custody (users typically do not self-custody within PayPal)", "Decentralization Impact": "Expands mainstream Ethereum access while introducing strong centralized custody and policy chokepoints", "Ethereum Support": "Ethereum Mainnet (direct support); limited L2 exposure via partners", "Failure Mode": "Crypto purchases restricted or disabled by jurisdiction or policy", "Founded Year": "1998", "Freeze / Denial Capability": "Yes", "Geographic Coverage": "Multi-Region (primarily U.S.; selective international rollout)", "Headquarters / Primary Jurisdiction": "United States", "Is Ethereum Required for Core Function?": "Partial (crypto is an extension of PayPal’s payments stack)", "KYC / AML Requirement": "Mandatory (PayPal account–level KYC)", "Maintaining Organization": "PayPal Holdings, Inc.", "Number of Countries Supported": "Limited (U.S.-first; expanding selectively)", "On-Ramp vs Exchange Bias": "Access-First (Payment Network–Embedded)", "Practitioner Validation Check": "Is this entity’s primary value proposition (for this product) enabling users to get fiat value into Ethereum-native assets? Yes", "Practitioner’s Note": "PayPal offers unmatched distribution and trust, but access is highly permissioned: custody, transfers, and feature availability are tightly controlled, making it a powerful but policy-constrained on-ramp.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Regulatory approvals, internal policy decisions, banking partners", "Primary Function": "Fiat → Crypto Access (Payment Network–Embedded)", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "United States (federal + state), EU (select markets)", "Primary Use Case": "Mainstream fiat onboarding into Ethereum exposure", "Primary User Segment": "Retail users; mainstream, non-crypto-native users", "Primary Value Flow": "Fiat (PayPal balance) → ETH / Stablecoin → Ethereum", "Reason for Inclusion": "PayPal qualifies as a Global On-Ramp because its crypto functionality is explicitly designed to convert fiat into Ethereum assets for a broad user base, acting as an access gateway rather than a trading venue.", "Regional Restrictions": "Yes (significant; availability varies sharply by country)", "Regulatory Model": "Fully Licensed / Regulated (payments and crypto services)", "Role Relative to Ethereum": "Access Gateway (Mass-Market Payment Network)", "Supported Crypto Assets": "ETH, select stablecoins (e.g., PYUSD), limited additional assets", "Supported Fiat Currencies": "USD (primary), limited support for other currencies depending on region", "Supported Payment Methods": "PayPal balance, linked bank accounts, debit cards", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum Access": "Medium–High (distribution-driven)"}, "source_label": "Global On-Ramps"}'::jsonb),
  ('stripe-crypto-on-ramp-apis', 'Stripe (Crypto On-Ramp APIs)', 'monetary-access-rails', 'global-on-ramps', 'https://stripe.com/crypto', ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "Stripe’s Crypto On-Ramp APIs enable platforms and applications to embed compliant fiat-to-Ethereum asset purchases directly into their products, leveraging Stripe’s global payments infrastructure and developer tooling.", "extras": {"Access Bottleneck Type": "Regulation + Banking Partners + Platform Policy", "Conversion Control Model": "Centralized", "Custody of Funds": "Temporary in-flow custody; delivery to self-custody wallets or application-defined destinations", "Decentralization Impact": "Expands Ethereum access through mainstream platforms while introducing strong centralized policy and compliance chokepoints", "Ethereum Support": "Ethereum Mainnet; selective Ethereum L2 support via partners", "Failure Mode": "Crypto on-ramp disabled regionally; payments continue without crypto", "Founded Year": "2010", "Freeze / Denial Capability": "Yes", "Geographic Coverage": "Global (payments footprint–dependent)", "Headquarters / Primary Jurisdiction": "United States", "Is Ethereum Required for Core Function?": "Partial (crypto is an extension of Stripe’s payments stack)", "KYC / AML Requirement": "Mandatory (Stripe-managed identity & compliance flows)", "Maintaining Organization": "Stripe, Inc.", "Number of Countries Supported": "40+ (crypto on-ramp availability narrower than payments footprint)", "On-Ramp vs Exchange Bias": "Access-First (Payments-Embedded, Developer-Led)", "Practitioner Validation Check": "Is this entity’s primary value proposition (for this product) enabling users to get fiat value into Ethereum-native assets? Yes", "Practitioner’s Note": "Stripe is best understood as distribution infrastructure: it brings crypto access to mainstream apps, but availability and asset scope are tightly gated by jurisdiction, compliance posture, and Stripe’s internal risk controls.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Regulatory approvals, banking partners, card networks, Stripe policy decisions", "Primary Function": "Fiat → Crypto Access (API-First Payments Infrastructure)", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "United States, EU, UK, select APAC markets", "Primary Use Case": "Embedded, compliant fiat onboarding into Ethereum within apps", "Primary User Segment": "Developers, platforms, marketplaces embedding crypto onboarding", "Primary Value Flow": "Fiat → ETH / USDC → Ethereum", "Reason for Inclusion": "Stripe qualifies as a Global On-Ramp because its crypto APIs are explicitly designed to convert fiat into Ethereum assets at scale for third-party platforms, with access as the primary value proposition rather than trading.", "Regional Restrictions": "Yes (crypto features gated by jurisdiction)", "Regulatory Model": "Licensed / Regulated payments provider with crypto-specific compliance layers", "Role Relative to Ethereum": "Access Gateway / Distribution Infrastructure", "Supported Crypto Assets": "ETH, major stablecoins (USDC), select ERC-20 assets", "Supported Fiat Currencies": "USD, EUR, GBP, and select local currencies (jurisdiction-dependent)", "Supported Payment Methods": "Cards (credit/debit), Bank Transfers (ACH, SEPA), local payment methods (limited)", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum Access": "Medium–High"}, "source_label": "Global On-Ramps"}'::jsonb),
  ('sardine', 'Sardine', 'monetary-access-rails', 'global-on-ramps', 'https://www.sardine.ai', ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "Sardine provides a compliance-first fiat-to-crypto access infrastructure layer, combining payments, KYC, AML, and fraud prevention to enable platforms to onboard users into Ethereum assets through regulated banking and card rails.", "extras": {"Access Bottleneck Type": "Regulation + Banking Partners + Fraud/Risk Controls", "Conversion Control Model": "Centralized", "Custody of Funds": "Temporary in-flow custody or partner custody (implementation-dependent)", "Decentralization Impact": "Improves institutional-grade access reliability while reinforcing centralized compliance and policy controls", "Ethereum Support": "Ethereum Mainnet; selective Ethereum L2s via partner integrations", "Failure Mode": "Onboarding blocked or transactions declined due to risk controls", "Founded Year": "2021", "Freeze / Denial Capability": "Yes (risk and fraud controls embedded)", "Geographic Coverage": "Multi-Region (U.S.-anchored; expanding internationally)", "Headquarters / Primary Jurisdiction": "United States", "Is Ethereum Required for Core Function?": "Partial (multi-chain support; Ethereum is a primary destination)", "KYC / AML Requirement": "Mandatory (real-time identity, fraud, and AML screening)", "Maintaining Organization": "Sardine, Inc.", "Number of Countries Supported": "30+ (approx., partner-dependent)", "On-Ramp vs Exchange Bias": "Access-First (Infrastructure / Compliance-Led)", "Practitioner Validation Check": "Is this entity’s primary value proposition enabling users to get fiat value into Ethereum-native assets? Yes", "Practitioner’s Note": "Sardine is best understood as risk and compliance infrastructure disguised as an on-ramp: it materially improves regulatory survivability but can increase friction and false negatives in user onboarding.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Banking partners, compliance policy, fraud model false positives", "Primary Function": "Fiat → Crypto Access (Compliance-Native Infrastructure)", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "United States (state-level MSB), select international partners", "Primary Use Case": "Regulated fiat onboarding with embedded fraud and AML controls", "Primary User Segment": "Fintech platforms, exchanges, wallets embedding compliant onboarding", "Primary Value Flow": "Fiat → ETH / USDC → Ethereum", "Reason for Inclusion": "Sardine qualifies as a Global On-Ramp because its primary value proposition is enabling compliant fiat → Ethereum asset conversion at multi-region scale, acting as access infrastructure rather than a trading venue.", "Regional Restrictions": "Yes (strong U.S. and jurisdictional gating)", "Regulatory Model": "Licensed / Partner-Licensed (compliance-first stack)", "Role Relative to Ethereum": "Access Gateway Infrastructure (Compliance & Risk Layer)", "Supported Crypto Assets": "ETH, major stablecoins (USDC), partner-supported assets", "Supported Fiat Currencies": "USD (primary), limited additional currencies via partners", "Supported Payment Methods": "Bank Transfers (ACH), Cards (credit/debit via partners)", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum Access": "Medium"}, "source_label": "Global On-Ramps"}'::jsonb),
  ('alchemy-pay', 'Alchemy Pay', 'monetary-access-rails', 'global-on-ramps', 'https://alchemypay.org', ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "Alchemy Pay is a global fiat-to-crypto on-ramp that specializes in connecting local payment methods and e-wallets to Ethereum assets, enabling users in emerging and underbanked regions to access crypto via familiar rails.", "extras": {"Access Bottleneck Type": "Local Payment Partners + Regulation + FX Settlement", "Conversion Control Model": "Centralized", "Custody of Funds": "Temporary in-flow custody; settlement via partners to user wallets", "Decentralization Impact": "Expands Ethereum access in emerging markets while introducing reliance on localized, centralized payment partners", "Ethereum Support": "Ethereum Mainnet; Ethereum L2s via supported assets and withdrawals", "Failure Mode": "Specific local rails disabled; country-level access interruption", "Founded Year": "2018", "Freeze / Denial Capability": "Yes", "Geographic Coverage": "Global (Asia-, LATAM-, and emerging-market–strong)", "Headquarters / Primary Jurisdiction": "Singapore (global operations)", "Is Ethereum Required for Core Function?": "Partial (multi-chain support; Ethereum is a primary destination)", "KYC / AML Requirement": "Mandatory; tiered based on payment method and jurisdiction", "Maintaining Organization": "Alchemy Pay", "Number of Countries Supported": "170+ (approx., local-rail dependent)", "On-Ramp vs Exchange Bias": "Access-First", "Practitioner Validation Check": "Is this entity’s primary value proposition enabling users to get fiat value into Ethereum-native assets? Yes", "Practitioner’s Note": "Alchemy Pay is strongest where card and bank penetration is low; practitioners should model local-rail dependency risk and FX settlement complexity alongside regulatory exposure.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Local payment partners, regulatory shifts, FX settlement", "Primary Function": "Fiat → Crypto Access (Local Rail–Optimized)", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "Singapore, EU (select), Asia-Pacific, LATAM (varies by rail)", "Primary Use Case": "Low-friction fiat onboarding via local payment methods", "Primary User Segment": "Retail users; merchants; platforms targeting emerging markets", "Primary Value Flow": "Fiat → Stablecoin / ETH → Ethereum", "Reason for Inclusion": "Alchemy Pay qualifies as a Global On-Ramp because its core value proposition is enabling multi-country fiat → Ethereum access using local payment infrastructure, acting as an access gateway rather than a settlement network.", "Regional Restrictions": "Yes (method- and jurisdiction-specific constraints)", "Regulatory Model": "Partner-Licensed / Hybrid (local payment partnerships)", "Role Relative to Ethereum": "Access Gateway / Distribution Layer", "Supported Crypto Assets": "ETH, major stablecoins (USDT, USDC), select ERC-20 tokens", "Supported Fiat Currencies": "USD, EUR, GBP, JPY, KRW, PHP, IDR, VND, BRL, and numerous local currencies", "Supported Payment Methods": "Credit/Debit Cards, Bank Transfers, Extensive Local Payment Rails (e-wallets, mobile money, QR systems)", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum Access": "Medium–High (emerging markets)"}, "source_label": "Global On-Ramps"}'::jsonb),
  ('socket-fiat-chain-routing', 'Socket (Fiat → Chain Routing)', 'monetary-access-rails', 'global-on-ramps', 'https://www.socket.tech', ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "Socket provides a chain-aware routing layer that orchestrates fiat-to-crypto onboarding by directing users through integrated on-ramps and bridging paths to land assets directly on the target Ethereum chain or L2.", "extras": {"Access Bottleneck Type": "Partner Availability + Routing Logic + Regulation (inherited)", "Conversion Control Model": "Partner-Dependent", "Custody of Funds": "None (routes users to third-party on-ramps; no fiat custody)", "Decentralization Impact": "Improves access efficiency and L2 adoption while adding an orchestration dependency layer", "Ethereum Support": "Ethereum Mainnet; Ethereum L2s (Arbitrum, Optimism, Base, Polygon)", "Failure Mode": "Fiat routing unavailable; degraded success rates or higher costs", "Founded Year": "2021", "Freeze / Denial Capability": "Indirect (via underlying on-ramp providers)", "Geographic Coverage": "Global (inherits coverage from integrated providers)", "Headquarters / Primary Jurisdiction": "United States (global operations)", "Is Ethereum Required for Core Function?": "Partial (multi-chain router; Ethereum and L2s are primary destinations)", "KYC / AML Requirement": "Mandatory (performed by underlying on-ramp providers)", "Maintaining Organization": "Socket Technologies", "Number of Countries Supported": "150+ (approx., partner-dependent)", "On-Ramp vs Exchange Bias": "Access-First (Orchestration / Routing Layer)", "Practitioner Validation Check": "Is this entity’s primary value proposition (for this use case) enabling users to get fiat value into Ethereum-native assets? Yes", "Practitioner’s Note": "Socket reduces post-onboarding friction by collapsing fiat purchase, bridging, and chain selection into a single flow, but introduces second-order dependency risk tied to partner availability and routing correctness.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Availability, pricing, and uptime of integrated on-ramps; routing logic failures", "Primary Function": "Fiat → Crypto Access Orchestration (Routing Layer)", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "Inherited from integrated providers (MoonPay, Ramp, Transak, Coinbase On-Ramp, etc.)", "Primary Use Case": "Chain-aware fiat onboarding; routing to optimal Ethereum L2", "Primary User Segment": "Wallets, dApps, and users optimizing cost and destination chain", "Primary Value Flow": "Fiat → (Partner On-Ramp) → Stablecoin / ETH → Target Ethereum Chain", "Reason for Inclusion": "Socket qualifies as a Global On-Ramp because, when used for fiat → chain routing, its primary value proposition is enabling users to enter Ethereum and its L2s efficiently, even though it does not directly process fiat.", "Regional Restrictions": "Yes (inherits jurisdictional limits from underlying on-ramps)", "Regulatory Model": "Partner-Licensed (does not directly custody or process fiat)", "Role Relative to Ethereum": "Access Orchestration Layer (Chain-Aware Router)", "Supported Crypto Assets": "ETH, major stablecoins (USDC, USDT), partner-supported assets", "Supported Fiat Currencies": "USD, EUR, GBP, and local currencies (via integrated on-ramps)", "Supported Payment Methods": "Cards, Bank Transfers, Local Payment Rails (via partners)", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum Access": "Medium"}, "source_label": "Global On-Ramps"}'::jsonb),
  ('circle-circle-payments-circle-apis', 'Circle (Circle Payments / Circle APIs)', 'monetary-access-rails', 'institutional-payment-rails', 'https://www.circle.com', ARRAY['Ethereum Mainnet', 'Ethereum L2s (e.g.', 'Arbitrum', 'Optimism', 'Base)']::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "Circle Payments and Circle APIs provide institutional-grade payment and settlement infrastructure using Ethereum-native stablecoins, enabling enterprises and fintechs to move value globally with programmable compliance and onchain finality while abstracting blockchain complexity from end users.", "extras": {"Abstraction Risk": "Ethereum-Abstracted", "Availability / SLA Expectations": "Enterprise-grade SLAs", "Custody Model": "Platform-managed with qualified custodians; enterprise self-custody options", "Ethereum Role in Stack": "Native settlement layer (primary)", "Failure Mode": "Settlement delays; jurisdictional restrictions; redemption throttling", "Founded Year": "2013", "Geographic Scope": "Global (jurisdiction-approved)", "Headquarters / Primary Jurisdiction": "United States", "Institutional Adoption Level": "High", "Interaction with DeFi": "Limited / programmatic (escrow, automation, select integrations)", "Interaction with On-Ramps": "Indirect (via partner integrations)", "Interaction with Stablecoins": "Primary settlement medium (issuer)", "Is Ethereum Abstracted from End Users?": "Yes (API- and workflow-driven abstraction)", "KYC / AML Enforcement Level": "Institutional-grade", "Maintaining Organization": "Circle Internet Financial, LLC", "Practitioner Validation Check": "Does this entity use Ethereum as a backend settlement layer for institutional-grade payment flows, even if users never touch Ethereum directly? Yes", "Practitioner’s Note": "Circle is the canonical institutional settlement layer on Ethereum: it combines regulatory trust, stablecoin issuance, and API abstraction, but concentrates systemic risk around issuer solvency, banking access, and regulatory posture.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Banking partners, regulatory approvals, stablecoin reserve management", "Primary Function": "Institutional stablecoin settlement; treasury and cross-border payments", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "United States (state-level MSB), EU (EMI framework), other approved jurisdictions", "Primary Use Case": "B2B settlement, cross-border payments, programmable treasury flows", "Programmability Level": "Conditional / Programmable", "Reason for Inclusion": "Circle qualifies as an Institutional Payment Rail because it uses Ethereum as the backend settlement layer for high-value, enterprise-grade payment flows, with stablecoins as the primary settlement asset and compliance-first APIs for institutions.", "Regulatory Posture": "Regulated / Licensed", "Settlement Asset Types": "Stablecoins (USDC, EURC)", "Settlement vs Access Classification": "Settlement Rail", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum": "High", "Target User Segment": "Enterprises, fintechs, financial institutions", "Transaction Scale": "High-value and high-volume"}, "source_label": "Institutional Payment Rails"}'::jsonb),
  ('tether-institutional-settlement-on-ethereum', 'Tether (Institutional Settlement on Ethereum)', 'monetary-access-rails', 'institutional-payment-rails', 'https://tether.to', ARRAY['Ethereum Mainnet', 'select Ethereum L2s (limited', 'asset-dependent)']::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], '{"description": "Tether facilitates institutional-grade payment and settlement flows on Ethereum through USDT, enabling high-value, cross-border transfers and liquidity provisioning with onchain finality, primarily serving exchanges, market makers, and global enterprises.", "extras": {"Abstraction Risk": "Ethereum-Visible / Partially Abstracted", "Availability / SLA Expectations": "Market-driven (no formal enterprise SLA; high uptime expectations)", "Custody Model": "Enterprise self-custody; third-party custodians; exchange-managed custody", "Ethereum Role in Stack": "Native settlement layer (primary for USDT-ERC20)", "Failure Mode": "Redemption disruption; jurisdictional banking access loss; counterparty freezes", "Founded Year": "2014", "Geographic Scope": "Global", "Headquarters / Primary Jurisdiction": "British Virgin Islands (global operations)", "Institutional Adoption Level": "High", "Interaction with DeFi": "Limited (used as liquidity and settlement asset; not treasury-native)", "Interaction with On-Ramps": "Indirect (via exchanges, OTC desks, payment partners)", "Interaction with Stablecoins": "Primary settlement medium (issuer)", "Is Ethereum Abstracted from End Users?": "Partial (institutions interact via wallets and APIs; blockchain visible but operationally abstracted)", "KYC / AML Enforcement Level": "Institutional-grade for direct counterparties; partner-enforced downstream", "Maintaining Organization": "Tether Holdings Ltd.", "Practitioner Validation Check": "Does this entity use Ethereum as a backend settlement layer for institutional-grade payment flows, even if users never touch Ethereum directly? Yes", "Practitioner’s Note": "Tether is systemically critical but structurally idiosyncratic: it dominates institutional settlement volume on Ethereum while introducing elevated issuer, regulatory, and banking-partner risk compared to fully regulated peers.", "Primary Dependency Risks": "Banking partners, regulatory enforcement, reserve transparency, issuer risk", "Primary Function": "Institutional stablecoin settlement; treasury and cross-border value transfer", "Primary Regulatory Jurisdictions": "BVI; compliance interfaces in U.S., EU, Asia via partners", "Primary Use Case": "High-value settlement, exchange liquidity provisioning, cross-border treasury flows", "Programmability Level": "Static Payments (limited programmability at issuer level)", "Reason for Inclusion": "Tether qualifies as an Institutional Payment Rail because USDT on Ethereum is extensively used for large-scale, institutional settlement flows where Ethereum provides the final settlement layer, even when operational processes are abstracted.", "Regulatory Posture": "Offshore / Partner-Regulated (jurisdictionally fragmented)", "Settlement Asset Types": "Stablecoins (USDT)", "Settlement vs Access Classification": "Settlement Rail", "Systemic Importance to Ethereum": "High", "Target User Segment": "Exchanges, market makers, OTC desks, enterprises, fintechs", "Transaction Scale": "High-value and high-volume"}, "source_label": "Institutional Payment Rails"}'::jsonb),
